{"causes":["The Brothers Grimm were influenced by earlier European folklore when creating Kinder- und Hausmärchen.","Rapunzel's story reflects the societal norms of 19th-century Germany, where women's roles were largely limited to domestic duties.","The tale was popularized through oral retellings and written publications during the Romantic era."],"effects":["Rapunzel has become a cultural icon, symbolizing femininity and creativity.","The story has influenced various adaptations in film, literature, and art.","Kinder- und Hausmärchen has been widely translated and remains a beloved fairy tale collection worldwide."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"wilhelm-grimm","sourceName":"Wilhelm Grimm","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"rapunzel","targetName":"Rapunzel","context":"The Brothers Grimm drew inspiration from European folklore when creating Kinder- und Hausmärchen."},{"sourceSlug":"german-folklore","sourceName":"German Folklore","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"rapunzel","targetName":"Rapunzel","context":"Rapunzel's story reflects the societal norms of 19th-century Germany."},{"sourceSlug":"romantic-era","sourceName":"Romantic Era","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"kinder-und-hausmaerchen","targetName":"Kinder- und Hausmärchen","context":"Rapunzel's story was popularized through oral retellings and written publications during the Romantic era."},{"sourceSlug":"germany","sourceName":"Germany","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"rapunzel","targetName":"Rapunzel","context":"Rapunzel has become a cultural icon, symbolizing femininity and creativity in German culture."},{"sourceSlug":"feminist-literature","sourceName":"Feminist Literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"rapunzel","targetName":"Rapunzel","context":"The story has influenced various adaptations in film, literature, and art."},{"sourceSlug":"childrens-literature","sourceName":"Children's Literature","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"kinder-und-hausmaerchen","targetName":"Kinder- und Hausmärchen","context":"Kinder- und Hausmärchen has been widely translated and remains a beloved fairy tale collection worldwide."}],"places":[{"name":"Germany","role":"IS_HOME_TO"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Rapunzel","type":"Reference article","slug":"rapunzel"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q273585","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapunzel"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Rapunzel contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Rapunzel is a German fairy tale first recorded in published form by Friedrich Schulz in his Kleine Romane (1790), under the title 'Rapunzel', based on a French source story by Charlotte-Rose de Caumont de La Force ('Persinette', 1698), which was itself derived from Giambattista Basile's 'Petrosinella' in the Pentamerone (Naples, 1634). The Brothers Grimm included Rapunzel as tale KHM 12 in their Kinder- und Hausmärchen (1st edition, 1812), and it is primarily through the Grimm version (revised in the 7th edition, 1857 — sanitising the original's references to Rapunzel's pregnancy) that the tale became internationally known. The tale narrates the story of a girl with extraordinarily long golden hair, imprisoned by an enchantress in a tall tower (without a door), who lets down her hair as a ladder for the enchantress (and, later, a prince) to climb — the prince's discovery, the enchantress's punishment (cutting Rapunzel's hair and banishing her to a desert), and the prince's blinding by thorns and eventual reunion with Rapunzel in the desert.

Rapunzel belongs to the ATU type 310 ('The Maiden in the Tower') folk tale category, which exists in multiple European and Middle Eastern versions — the most widely distributed motif is of a beautiful girl imprisoned in an inaccessible tower. The tale's central symbol — the hair as rope, the tower as prison, the cutting of hair as punishment and liberation — has generated rich literary and feminist analysis: the tower as patriarchal enclosure, the hair as female sexuality and power, and Rapunzel's relationship with the enchantress as a complex of female power, dependence, and control.

Rapunzel's global cultural reach in the 21st century is largely the product of Disney's Tangled (2010) — a computer-animated adaptation that reimagined Rapunzel as an adventurous protagonist and the tower as protective enclosure rather than prison, grossing $590 million worldwide and establishing Rapunzel as a major Disney Princess figure. The tale has also been the subject of numerous literary retellings, from Angela Carter's feminist reimagining to Donna Jo Napoli's novel Zel (1996).","subjects":["German Literature","19th Century","Fairy Tale","Brothers Grimm","Folk Literature","Disney Animation","Feminist Criticism","Children's Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Rapunzel's significance extends beyond its entertainment value, offering a window into the historical context in which it was written.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
